window.__SEARCH_INDEX__=[{"t":"About me","p":"About me","u":"About/AboutMe.html","x":"Engineer by profession, Educator by passion Hi, I'm Vaisakh. I build multi-domain physical system simulations from first principles, contribute to open-source modeling standards, and break down complex engineering topics into practical tutorials. Whether I’m modeling transient thermal-fluid networks in Modelica, coupling multi-FMU co-simulations, or scripting local-first AI tools in Python, I care deeply about transparent, deterministic engineering over black-box shortcuts. Outside of day-to-day engineering work, I run Simulation Workshop—an educational channel and documentation hub dedicated to helping engineers master multi-domain system modeling—and actively contribute to the Modelica Standard Library ecosystem. ⚙️ System Modeling Thermal-fluid networks, 1D finite volume heat transfer, magnetic circuits, and SSP/FMU co-simulation workflows. 🎥 Simulation Workshop Creating deep-dive video walkthroughs and open documentation that bridge theoretical equations with live OpenModelica code. 🛠️ Open-Source & Local AI Contributing to Modelica libraries and building lightweight Python pipelines and local LLM utilities on Linux. Find me at LinkedIn Checkout on YouTube Find with me on GitHub"},{"t":"Bridging the gap between physics and Mathematics in Modelica","p":"Bridging the gap between physics and Mathematics in Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Articles/TypesInModelica.html","x":"Units and Dimensions bring physical meaning to mathematical quantities. The number 4 means widely different things depending on whether it is 4m length, 4kg mass or 4C temperature. As Simulations stand on the intersection of physical world and mathematical algorithms, efficient management of units is vital for accurate modeling of physical systems using mathematical frameworks. ​ Modelica provides an effective way to handle physical quantities using types. Modelica has primitive types such as: Real Integer String Boolean enumeration On top of these, Modelica provides derived types that Inherit from the primitive types. Majority of physical quantities are derived from Real types as they have to represent continuously varying values. Derived types enforce: ​Dimensional Consistency across equations This is a valid equation $$ \\text{Velocity} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{displacement} $$ This is not a valid equation $$ Temperature + Pressure = Energy $$ ​Define range and default values to quantities to improve solver stability, physical consistency and drag and drop implementation of components. ​"},{"t":"Additional key attributes that make Modelica types even more powerful:","p":"Bridging the gap between physics and Mathematics in Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Articles/TypesInModelica.html#additional-key-attributes-that-make-modelica-types-even-more-powerful","x":"DisplayUnit & quantity: Decouples the underlying SI unit used in numerical solver calculations from the units shown in GUI dialogs and plot axes. Nominal: Tells non-linear solvers the expected scale/magnitude of a variable, ensuring balanced numerical precision across vastly different physical scales. The key takeway Modelica at its core is a mathematical equation solver and features like units and dimensions provide the physical guard rails to keep the simulation under realistic conditions"},{"t":"Introduction to Modelica Language","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#introduction-to-modelica-language","x":"It is tempting to get started with a new tool by writting something and see the results. But I feel it is important to acknoledge the history and the milestones of its development. This video covers the why and how of Modelica's evolution, the concept of modeling physical systems through equations and an introduction to Open Modelica ecosystem. Introduction to Modelica"},{"t":"Building your first model from scratch - Modelica Basics","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#building-your-first-model-from-scratch-modelica-basics","x":"Transient heating and cooling of a metal ball can be simulated by incorporating the relationship between heat and temperature along with Newton's law of cooling. This is an introductory course in Modelica where we will build our own simulation model using equations. Building your first model"},{"t":"Modelica Standard Library - The power of pre-validated models","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#modelica-standard-library-the-power-of-pre-validated-models","x":"Revisiting the previous problem statement of heating and cooling a metal ball, but this time we will use the components from Modelica standard library to generate the exact same results as before. We understand the purpose of using library components instead of building everything from scratch. Modelica Standard Library"},{"t":"Building Your own models and connecting them to to MSL Components","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#building-your-own-models-and-connecting-them-to-to-msl-components","x":"Both approaches of building components from scratch and using Modelica standard library has its on pros and cons. So why not get the best of both worlds buy building our own components that can interact with other components from the standard library. We build our custom ball model and an air stream component and make them interact with each other. Custom components"},{"t":"Building an Electrical Kettle in Modelica - Introudction to multi-domain simulations","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#building-an-electrical-kettle-in-modelica-introudction-to-multi-domain-simulations","x":"Most of the engineered products are a combination of multiple physical domains and their interactions. We model an electrical kettle incorporating the electrical, Thermal and control aspects of it. We understand the process of iterative modeling. Multi-domain modeling"},{"t":"Fluid Property Models in Modelica - Thermodynamics in Simulations","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#fluid-property-models-in-modelica-thermodynamics-in-simulations","x":"Fluid Flow forms a foundational part in modeling of engineering systems. Thanks to Modelica media library, we have an extensive list of media packages under the hood. We will learn what are fluid property models, how to use them and eventually perform a thermodynamic analysis of a power plant using it. Fluid Property models"},{"t":"Modeling a magnetic circuit in Modelica","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html#modeling-a-magnetic-circuit-in-modelica","x":"Introduction to electromagnetism in modelica by solving a simple textbook problem on electrical machinery. Electrical machinery introduction"},{"t":"Very short Explainers and Tips","p":"Video Tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Video-Tutorials.html","x":"parameters Variability"},{"t":"Units and Dimensions handling in Modelica","p":"Written tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Written-Tutorials.html#units-and-dimensions-handling-in-modelica","x":"flowchart LR; A(Numerical Value) --> B(Units) --> C(Physical Dimensions) --> D(Consistant Equations) --> E(Stable Simulations) As Simulations stand on the intersection of physical world and mathematical algorithms, efficient management of units is vital for accurate modeling of physical systems using mathematical frameworks. Read a quick writeup on how Modelica handles physical units and dimensions into its simulation eco-system."},{"t":"Modelica Quiz","p":"Written tutorials on Modelica","u":"Modelica-tutorials/Written-Tutorials.html#modelica-quiz","x":"Find multiple choice questions to test your knowledge on Modelica language Quiz Here"},{"t":"Quick start","p":"Simulation workshop","u":"index.html#quick-start","x":"This is a very basic Modelica code to simulate the motion of a spring - mass - damper system. Check out more amazing content at the tutorial sections or find out how to get started below. model simpleSpringMass parameter Real M = 5; parameter Real k = 12; parameter Real C = 8; Real x; Real v; initial equation x = 1; equation der(x) = v; M*der(v) + C*v + k*x = 0; annotation( experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 10, Tolerance = 1e-06, Interval = 0.002)); end simpleSpringMass; Are You new to Modelica ? These are the places to check out for more content Modelica Official Website : modelica.org OpenModelica Official Website : openmodelica.org Modelica by Example by Dr. Michael M. Tiller : Modelica by Example OMPython - A python wrapper for OpenModelica compiler :OMPython FMpy - For running FMUs generated from Modelica in python : FMPY Simulation Workshop Youtube Channel : Simulation Workshop"},{"t":"Models Vs Simulations","p":"Models Vs Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/ModelVsSimulation.html","x":"What is the difference between a model and a simulation? Modeling and simulation are often mentioned together in several contexts just like \"Science & Technology\" Science Vs Technology Science gives us the governing principles of systems around us and technology consume this knowledge to serve specific practical purpose Though these are closely connected topics, they are different and and serve different purpose. \"Model\" is an abstract mathematical description of a \"system\". It usually consist of a set of variables and equations connecting them. These can be \"empirical\" - Derived from experiments or observations, or \"physics-based\" - derived from the governing principles in mechanics, thermodynamics, electromagnetism etc. Empirical models are computationally simpler and provides good accuracy within the the training data. But these are not extensible and have limited scope when parameters are changed. These are used either when the physics is too complex/unknown or the usability demands high accuracy in a limited range + sufficient data is available.. Physics-based models are more general and capture the fundamental characteristic of the system. These are mathematically complex to solve. They provide reasonable accuracy over a wide range of parameter variations. These are excellent for modeling inter-connected complex systems for exploring new concepts where high quality experiment data is not available. Hybrid-models often represents a combination of both worlds where the model is based on physics principles but certain key parameters like \" heat transfer coefficient\", \"coefficient of friction\", etc are empirically derived. \"Simulation\" is a virtual experiment performed by consuming any of the above models to solve a design problem or decide operational strategies. Simulations consume 1 or more models in an environment and execute numerical solvers. They solve the set of equations defined the models involved in it to calculate parameter values over a period of time defined in the simulation. Simulations are unique experiments and cater to a specific need. One need to make sure that the models used in the simulation are capable of serving the purpose of simulation. Happy Simulations !!!!"},{"t":"What is a simulation model?","p":"What is a simulation model?","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/WhatIsAModel.html","x":"Often in engineering simulations, I get asked: \"We have a component model, but why can’t we simulate this phenomenon?\" Many assume a mathematical model fully describes reality and captures every physical effect. But models are only as complete as the physics included. Take a resistor. Ohm’s law models it simply: voltage drop is proportional to current. This steady-state model ignores conditions. But resistance rises with temperature, so we need thermal equations. Current then heats the resistor, temperature rises, resistance changes, and current drops. Now it’s a coupled thermal-electrical problem requiring mass, heat capacity, and temp-resistance data. Heat also dissipates to surroundings via convection and conduction. Without modeling this, we get unrealistic temperatures. Adding transient heat storage gives a dynamic model responding to electrical heating and cooling. But failure prediction still isn’t possible. That needs material stress models, plasticity, melting, or combustion. Over time, oxidation alters thermal and electrical behavior, which adds chemical kinetics. None of these phenomena are independent but form a coupled system of equations, and accuracy depends on precise material and structural data. Even one Wrong input can break the whole system. Even so, such a model only gives deterministic results—the resistor fails the same way every single time. Reliability studies require stochastic models where key properties vary randomly, reflecting real-world variability. The takeaway: you can’t study resistor failure with just Ohm’s law. But you also don’t always need full fidelity models—if you just want circuit current, the simple one works. Worse, complex models with poor input data may be less accurate. So next time before picking a simulation tool or model, ask: \"What problem am I solving?\" \"Does my model have the right complexity?\" \"Do I have enough information?\" \"What assumptions am I making, and do they fit?\" \"What level of accuracy is realistic with my inputs?\" As a rule, start simple, validate, then add complexity step by step with a modular architecture, keeping models testable and flexible.Happy simulations !!!!!!!!"},{"t":"Story Telling through Simulations","p":"Story Telling through Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/storyTelling.html","x":"Modern simulation tools make it almost effortless to set up a model, run it, and process the outputs. Yet, many get stuck at the final step—interpreting the results, explaining their meaning, and deciding on the next course of action. Simulations never reveal the complete reality; they merely produce solutions to the equations we define, shaped entirely by our inputs and assumptions. A good way to approach simulations is to think of them as telling a story."},{"t":"Setting the Stage","p":"Story Telling through Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/storyTelling.html#setting-the-stage","x":"Every story begins with a reason for its existence, and every simulation must start with a strong problem statement. Ask yourself: What problem am I solving with this simulation? What outputs am I expecting from it? This sets your purpose and frames the rest of the work. Clarify your thinking early. sometimes a quick calculation can clarify your thinking. If the starting point is weak, the rest of the story will struggle to convince."},{"t":"Developing the Characters","p":"Story Telling through Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/storyTelling.html#developing-the-characters","x":"Characters are what drive a story forward. In the context of simulations, your characters are the models and inputs you provide. These must be logical, consistent, and as detailed as possible—because if your audience isn’t convinced by your setup, they won’t trust your results. Take time to justify your choices, verify their plausibility, and ensure that everything aligns with the problem you are trying to solve."},{"t":"Navigating the Plot","p":"Story Telling through Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/storyTelling.html#navigating-the-plot","x":"The plot is advanced by interactions, choices, and constraints—just as the solution method guides the model toward results. Ask yourself whether your solver is capable of handling the equations you’ve defined. Consider its level of accuracy, robustness, and ability to handle corner cases. Make sure you have the right inputs and all necessary data to actually solve the problem. If any of these points fail, you may need to revisit and adjust your model before moving forward."},{"t":"Reaching the Climax","p":"Story Telling through Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/storyTelling.html#reaching-the-climax","x":"A compelling climax brings all elements of a story together, revealing the total picture. In simulations, this moment happens in post-processing, when the raw outputs are transformed into meaningful insights. Here, it is critical to connect the dots between assumptions, methods, and results. Present them in a way that answers your initial problem statement convincingly. Engage with your target audience or stakeholders to understand what they want from the results, draw on previous studies for comparison, and test your model’s behavior using “what if” questions."},{"t":"Closing the Story","p":"Story Telling through Simulations","u":"systemsimulation/Articles/storyTelling.html#closing-the-story","x":"Finally, think about how you will present your findings. Whether it is a detailed report, a long presentation, a short slide deck, or even an email, the format should match the needs of your audience. Keep the context intact while adjusting depth and detail. The best stories leave room for sequels—so highlight areas left unexplored, explain why those gaps exist, and suggest possible directions for future work. A good simulation, like a good story, should leave the audiance both satisfied and curious."},{"t":"Open Modelica Consortium","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#open-modelica-consortium","x":""},{"t":"Open Modelica Connection Editor","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#open-modelica-connection-editor","x":""},{"t":"OM Notebook","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#om-notebook","x":""},{"t":"Open Modelica Compiler","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#open-modelica-compiler","x":""},{"t":"OMPython - Running Simulations through a program","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#ompython-running-simulations-through-a-program","x":""},{"t":"FMU Export and Import - The FMI Standard","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#fmu-export-and-import-the-fmi-standard","x":""},{"t":"OMSimulator - Running FMUs through python","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#omsimulator-running-fmus-through-python","x":""},{"t":"Orchestrating FMUs - System Structure and Parameterization","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#orchestrating-fmus-system-structure-and-parameterization","x":""},{"t":"Open Modelica Online","p":"Introduction to Open Modelica Eco-system","u":"systemsimulation/Introduction_openModelica.html#open-modelica-online","x":""},{"t":"What is a Simulation Model","p":"Articles on Technology and Simulation","u":"systemsimulation/OtherArticles.html#what-is-a-simulation-model","x":"Explores the concept of model complexity and the purpose of building simulation models What is a simulation model"},{"t":"Difference between model & Simulation","p":"Articles on Technology and Simulation","u":"systemsimulation/OtherArticles.html#difference-between-model-simulation","x":"Discussion on how a model is different from a simulation to demistify the ambiguity between the two terms. Difference Between model & Simulation"},{"t":"Story Telling Through Simulations","p":"Articles on Technology and Simulation","u":"systemsimulation/OtherArticles.html#story-telling-through-simulations","x":"Human beings communicate ideas through story telling and simultion is not different. Explore how to plan a simulation study around a story that can help the stakeholders to make meaningful decisions. Story Telling Through Simulations"},{"t":"Quiz on Modelica","p":"Quiz on Modelica","u":"systemsimulation/Quiz/Quiz.html","x":"What is the name of modeling approach where relationship between variables are non-directional? Acausal modeling Parametric modeling Iterative modeling What should be the relation between number of variables and number of equations in a model? Number of variables should always be greater than equations They should always be equal There are no such relationships Check answers"}];